#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# coefficient

#% text
# `coefficient(p, x, k)` gives the coefficient of `x^k` in the polynomial `p`.

#% text
# `coefficient(p, x)` gives the coefficient of `x`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `p` is expanded first. Other symbols are parameters: the coefficient may be an expression in them.

#% item
# `k = 0` gives the terms free of `x`.

#% item
# With a form `x^s` of symbolic exponent, a power `x^(k s + c)` counts as `(x^s)^k x^c`: `coefficient(x^(1 + s), x^s)` is `x`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
coefficient(3*x^2 + 2*x + 1, x, 2)
#% output
#= 3

#% cell
coefficient((x + 1)^5, x, 3)
#% output
#= 10

#% cell
coefficient(a*x^2 + b*x + c, x)
#% output
#= b

#% cell
coefficient(a*x^2 + b*x + c, x, 0)
#% output
#= c

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A coefficient that is itself a polynomial in another variable:

#% cell
coefficient((x + y)^4, x, 2)
#% output
#= 6*y^2

#% text
# The coefficient of `x*y`, one variable at a time:

#% cell
coefficient(coefficient(expand((x + y + 1)^3), x), y)
#% output
#= 6

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The coefficients are the elements of `coefficient_list`:

#% cell
p = 2 - 3*x + x^3; [coefficient(p, x, 3), coefficient_list(p, x)[4]]
#% output
#= [1, 1]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `coefficient_list`, `degree`, `expand`
